{"timestamp":"2026-09-23T23:50:16.403Z","ordinal":90545,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"019f3c64-2576-70c2-90c0-e6b212f79ee1","turn_id":"01a0d09d-47a5-7db3-b179-80b63f2ed88a","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_096c433c75860d41016ab465b1200c81918a694cac5c2d60f2","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Your instinct was partly right. In Site, the root Alchemy file is a thin entrypoint and the infrastructure package owns the resource declarations. Bundjil had those declarations in a separate root `stacks/` folder. I moved them into [the infrastructure package](/Users/cooper/Projects/bundjil/packages/infrastructure/src/stack.ts), matching Site’s ownership pattern.\n\nI also removed a test that pinned exact wording in old documents, removed a check that hardcoded one past merge receipt, and stopped replaying a historical evaluation on every CI run. CI now checks out only the current commit. I kept the checks for current code, credentials, deployment order and rollback. Bundjil still needs more infrastructure code than Site because it deals with both Vercel and Photon, plus adopted resources and drift reports; I did not collapse those distinct jobs just to make the folders look alike.\n\n**Verification:** the full repository checks and build passed using synthetic test credentials. The generic package-structure validator reported ignored `.local` reference clones and `.turbo` output, so I’m relying on Bundjil’s own checks for this change. The edits are local and uncommitted; nothing was deployed or pushed.\n\nThe next worthwhile tidy-up is the infrastructure package’s large export file. That needs its own careful pass so scripts do not lose imports they use.\n\n"}],"phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":{"entries":[{"path":"MEMORY.md","lineStart":663,"lineEnd":674,"note":"Site Alchemy cleanup context used to scope the comparison"}],"rolloutIds":["01a084be-66a8-7943-a7bf-55e326d0bea5"]}},"started_at_ms":1790207409154,"completed_at_ms":1790207416403}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-23T23:50:16.407Z","ordinal":90546,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_096c433c75860d41016ab465b1200c81918a694cac5c2d60f2","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Your instinct was partly right. In Site, the root Alchemy file is a thin entrypoint and the infrastructure package owns the resource declarations. Bundjil had those declarations in a separate root `stacks/` folder. I moved them into [the infrastructure package](/Users/cooper/Projects/bundjil/packages/infrastructure/src/stack.ts), matching Site’s ownership pattern.\n\nI also removed a test that pinned exact wording in old documents, removed a check that hardcoded one past merge receipt, and stopped replaying a historical evaluation on every CI run. CI now checks out only the current commit. I kept the checks for current code, credentials, deployment order and rollback. Bundjil still needs more infrastructure code than Site because it deals with both Vercel and Photon, plus adopted resources and drift reports; I did not collapse those distinct jobs just to make the folders look alike.\n\n**Verification:** the full repository checks and build passed using synthetic test credentials. The generic package-structure validator reported ignored `.local` reference clones and `.turbo` output, so I’m relying on Bundjil’s own checks for this change. The edits are local and uncommitted; nothing was deployed or pushed.\n\nThe next worthwhile tidy-up is the infrastructure package’s large export file. That needs its own careful pass so scripts do not lose imports they use.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:663-674|note=[Site Alchemy cleanup context used to scope the comparison]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a084be-66a8-7943-a7bf-55e326d0bea5\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0d09d-47a5-7db3-b179-80b63f2ed88a","content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}}}
